menu "Free Harvest Adapter"

choice HR_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default HR_BOARD_DEVKITC
    help
        Which board the firmware is built for. The protocol, web UI, MQTT
        and capture log are identical on every board; the choice only adds
        the peripherals a particular board carries. The default keeps the
        build exactly as it always was for a bare ESP32-S3 DevKitC.

config HR_BOARD_DEVKITC
    bool "ESP32-S3 DevKitC-1 (no display, no LED, no button)"

config HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    bool "LilyGo T-Dongle-S3 (0.96\" ST7735 LCD, APA102 LED, BOOT button)"
    help
        USB-A stick with an 80x160 colour LCD, one RGB LED and one button.
        Note the board has a single USB port that TinyUSB takes over for the
        dryer, so the serial console is only reachable on UART0 (GPIO43/44,
        the QWIIC connector on recent revisions) or through /api/log.

endchoice

config HR_DISPLAY
    bool "Status display on the on-board LCD"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Show the cycle phase, temperature, vacuum and connection state on the
        LCD. Purely an output: nothing on the display path can send a frame
        to the dryer.

config HR_LED
    bool "Mirror the state on the RGB LED"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Colour = phase, blink/breathe = attention needed. Brightness is capped
        at 30 % because the LED sits inside a closed case next to the antenna.

config HR_BUTTON
    bool "Use the BOOT button to switch screens / toggle the backlight"
    depends on HR_BOARD_T_DONGLE_S3
    default y
    help
        Short press: dismiss an alert, or cycle main -> info -> raw frame.
        Long press (1.5 s): backlight and LED off/on. The button never sends
        anything to the dryer - a stray press on a stick protruding from the
        machine must not be able to start or stop a batch.

config HR_DISPLAY_ROTATE_180
    bool "Rotate the display 180 degrees (USB plug on the right)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY
    default y
    help
        USB port of the Harvest Right dryer is on the right side; rotate so
        the screen reads upright when plugged in. Disable this for a port
        on the left (the vendor sketch's orientation). Only the panel's
        MADCTL changes; the frame buffer, fonts, button and LED are
        unaffected.

config HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    bool "Dim, then switch off the backlight when nothing happens"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY
    default y
    help
        The stick sits in the dryer around the clock. After HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S
        without an event the backlight ramps down to HR_DISPLAY_DIM_PCT
        (the screen keeps updating); after HR_DISPLAY_OFF_S the backlight
        and the panel are switched off. Events that wake the screen: a
        button press, the dryer changing phase (start, freeze -> dry,
        complete), an alert, the USB link dropping or returning, Wi-Fi or
        MQTT state changes. Plain telemetry updates (temperature, vacuum,
        elapsed time) do not, or the screen would never dim during a run.
        The IPS panel has no burn-in; this is about heat, backlight life
        and glare at night. Disable to keep the old always-on behaviour.

config HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S
    int "Dim after (seconds of inactivity)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 5 86400
    default 30

config HR_DISPLAY_OFF_S
    int "Switch the screen off after (seconds of inactivity, 0 = never)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 0 604800
    default 300
    help
        Counted from the last event, like HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S, so it should be
        larger than that; a value at or below HR_DISPLAY_DIM_S skips the
        DIM stage. 0 keeps the screen dimmed but never off. While the
        setup access point is open the screen never goes off: the user is
        reading the network name and address off it. When the screen is
        off, the first button press only wakes it (nothing else happens).

config HR_DISPLAY_DIM_PCT
    int "Dimmed backlight (%)"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    range 1 99
    default 10

config HR_DISPLAY_ALERT_KEEP_ON
    bool "Keep the screen at full brightness while an alert is showing"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM
    default y
    help
        An alert that has not been acknowledged (link lost mid-run, dryer
        diagnostics, bad frames...) holds the screen ACTIVE. Disable to let
        it dim; it never goes off while an alert is active either way, and
        an acknowledged alert (status-bar "!") allows DIM but not OFF.

config HR_LED_DIM_WITH_DISPLAY
    bool "Dim the RGB LED as well when the screen is off"
    depends on HR_DISPLAY_AUTO_DIM && HR_LED
    default y
    help
        With the screen off the LED keeps mirroring the machine state, but
        capped at a low, still visible level (5 %) instead of its normal
        10-30 %. Disable to keep the LED at full pattern brightness.

config HR_AP_SSID
    string "Setup access-point SSID"
    default "HR-Adapter-Setup"
    help
        SSID broadcast when no WiFi credentials are stored (or the stored
        network can't be joined). Connect to it to reach the setup page.

config HR_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Setup access-point password (blank = open)"
    default ""
    help
        Password for the setup AP. Leave blank for an open network. If set,
        must be at least 8 characters (WPA2 requirement).

config HR_ACK_PAYLOAD
    string "GOTIT ack payload (UNVERIFIED)"
    default "ESP32ADAPTER"
    help
        Value sent in the GOTIT acknowledgement. The genuine adapter's
        payload is not yet known - capture it with a USB sniffer and set it
        here (or leave for later; the decoding UI works regardless).

config HR_HTTP_LOG_TO_UART
    bool "Also echo received frames to the UART console"
    default y

config HR_LOG_RING_KB
    int "In-app log ring (/api/log) size, KB"
    range 4 128
    default 32
    help
        Static DRAM reserved for the in-app copy of the ESP-IDF log that
        /api/log serves. Lines are stored packed (a line costs its own
        length plus two bytes), so 32 KB keeps roughly 500 lines of the
        ~65-character average - a boot, the handshake and several minutes
        of a busy link. Boards without PSRAM that carry other RAM users can
        turn this down; the ring only ever drops its oldest lines.

config HR_CAPTURE_RAW
    bool "Capture log: keep every frame verbatim (no repeat collapsing)"
    default n
    help
        By default consecutive identical frames (an idle dryer's REQINFO
        every 10 s) are collapsed into one "repeat ... xN" summary line to
        save flash. Enable this when recording traffic for protocol work,
        where the exact cadence of every frame matters more than space.
        On a 16 MB board the 11.9 MB capture partition holds several days
        of raw traffic even so.

endmenu
